Z||000000||Sndfr||12-06-2000||11:03 AM||||Is an engine swap of an '85 v6 engine with a manual transmission to an '84 SE (4 cyl automatic) possible in <I>any</I> way?<P>------------------<BR>-<B>matthew "Whyt Nyte" d.</B><BR>84 SE (stock, 4 cyl automatic)<BR><I>"You are not special.<BR>You are not a beatiful and unique snowflake.<BR>You are the same decaying organic matter as everyone else."</I><p>[This message has been edited by Sndfr (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||TheFiero64||12-06-2000||11:39 AM||||yes, but you must change all the wiring from the dash back.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sndfr||12-06-2000||12:30 PM||||Why?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Rodrv6||12-06-2000||04:12 PM||||The 84's were not offered with the V-6 and several things were changed in 85 in order to make the engine fit into the engine bay.  One of the big changes was the routing of the wiring harness in the engine bay.  The 84's have a rather large plug located on the firewall in front of the engine.  This is the junction between the tail light harness, the engine harness (oil pressure, tach, ignition and such) and the main harness that runs under the console and branches out behind the dash.  This junction plug is in the way of the forward exhaust manifold on the V-6.  Starting in 85 the plug was relocated to the right side of the engine bay next to the battery.  The routing of the tail light harness was also changed.  84's run around the left side of the engine bay to the back end.  85 and up run around the right side to the back.  You will have to relocate this plug on the 84.  The easiest way is probably to replace the main and rear end harness with the ones from the V6 donor car.  This is one reason why the 84's are the most difficult to convert although not impossible.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting :)||||reg||4
